<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad III of Granada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1257">1257</a>–<a href="/years/1314">1314</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasrid-dynasty">Nasrid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad III was the third Nasrid emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, reigning from 1302 to 1309. A patron of building, he constructed the main mosque of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a>. His capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> in 1306 helped provoke an alliance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, Aragon and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a> against Granada, and in 1309 a palace revolt deposed him in favour of his brother Nasr; he died in confinement in 1314.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muhammad was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> on 15 August 1257, in the reign of his grandfather Muhammad I, the founder of the dynasty. His father, the future Muhammad II, encouraged learning in his children: Muhammad gave himself to poetry, his sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatima">Fatima</a> studied the lives of Islamic scholars, and their much younger half-brother Nasr astronomy. As heir he took part in affairs of state, and he once nearly had his father's secretary Ibn al-Hakim executed over satirical verses wrongly attributed to him. He came to the throne at about 45, on 8 April 1302; the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-khatib">Ibn al-Khatib</a> cites a rumour, never confirmed, that he had poisoned his father.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Muhammad at first kept up his father's war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and his alliances with Aragon and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a>. His troops took Bedmar, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaen">Jaén</a>, two weeks after his accession, and in 1303 he suppressed a revolt by his relative Abu al-Hajjaj ibn Nasr, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadix">Guadix</a>. In August 1303, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, he made a three-year peace with Castile that confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>'s conquests, including Bedmar, Alcaudete and Quesada, in return for his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> to Ferdinand IV and the payment of tribute. The peace angered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volunteers-of-the-faith">Volunteers of the Faith</a>, North African warriors of holy war, and he dismissed 6,000 of his North African troops.</p><p>His failing sight left power increasingly with Ibn al-Hakim, who from 1303 held both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizierate</a> and the chancery and was styled holder of the two vizierates. The occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> and the Marinid ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ksar-es-seghir">Ksar es-Seghir</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larache">Larache</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asilah">Asilah</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a>, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> command of the Strait. On 19 December 1308 Aragon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> agreed at Alcalá de Henares to conquer and divide Granada, and Aragon made a pact with the Marinid sultan Abu al-Rabi to retake Ceuta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The coup of 14 March 1309, on the feast of Eid al-Fitr, was led by Atiq ibn al-Mawl, who killed Ibn al-Hakim, while the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> sacked the palaces of sultan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>. Muhammad abdicated before jurists and lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almunecar">Almuñécar</a>. After plots to restore him he was held in the Dar al-Kubra of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a> and drowned in one of its pools on 21 January 1314, probably at Nasr's order. He was known as al-Makhlu, the Deposed. Ibn al-Khatib tells of his cruelty to his father's household troops, but also preserves his poems, and the Partal Palace and a bathhouse that endowed the Alhambra's mosque were his work.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad III of Granada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.18,-3.6&amp;year=1294" aria-label="Granada on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4460 825 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Muhammad III of Granada"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4910 1095h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4910 1095h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.18,-3.6&amp;year=1294">What was here in 1294? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
